WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.470
Sadie Pfeifer was 9 years old when this photo
was taken.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:12.520
Operating heavy machinery that’s nearly
twice her height in a cotton mill in Lancaster,

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.549
South Carolina, in 1908.

00:00:14.549 --> 00:00:21.230
She was just one of many children working
in mills, fields, factories, and

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.480
mines.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.859
And although these kids were spread across
the United States, working in separate industries,

00:00:27.859 --> 00:00:30.650
they all had one thing in common:

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.060
They all met Lewis Hine.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:40.330
At the turn of the 20th century, the United
States knew it had a child labor problem.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:46.410
The 1900 federal census revealed that 1.75
million children under the age of 16, more

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.120
than one in five, were working at this time.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.250
The Industrial Revolution had mechanized American
and European manufacturing, and a cheap labor

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:58.440
force was needed to complete repetitive tasks
for hours on end.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.729
Children from poor families were targeted
for these jobs because they would work for

00:01:01.729 --> 00:01:05.510
next to nothing and were less likely to strike
than adults.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:09.871
State legislatures and the American public
knew this was happening on a mass scale, but

00:01:09.871 --> 00:01:11.390
didn’t act.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.680
Until they saw what it actually looked like.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.140
Starting in 1908, the newly formed National
Child Labor Committee hired a photographer

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.970
to investigate and report on the industries
employing children.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:30.040
That photographer was Lewis Wickes Hine: educator,
sociologist, and member of the Progressive Movement.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.220
A period in the United States that saw a wave
of political activism and social reform.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:39.990
Hine emphasized the potential power of photography
as a tool for social reform in a speech he

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:44.290
gave in 1909 called “Social photography:
how the camera may help.”

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:48.880
He said, “The dictum, then, of the social
worker is “Let there be light;” and in

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.780
this campaign for light we have for our advance
agent the light writer — the photograph.”

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.890
He traveled extensively, gathering information,
interviews, and images of working children

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.840
across the country.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.030
He visited coal mines in Pennsylvania.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:07.450
Where adolescent “breaker boys” worked
underground for hours, separating impurities

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:08.590
from coal.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:11.980
Sardine cutters in Maine.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:16.340
Oyster shuckers in Louisiana, some as young
as 4 years old.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.340
Tobacco pickers in Kentucky.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.620
Cranberry pickers in Massachusetts.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.660
Beet farms in Colorado.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:26.840
And young messengers and newsboys in cities
all over the country.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.580
Many of the photos captured adults nearby,
supervising the children while they worked.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:35.530
When Hine wasn’t allowed access to the mills
and factories, he waited outside

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.420
and documented the comings and goings of its
workers,

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.060
whose shifts often lasted late into the night.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.590
Laborers would pose for portraits and tell
Hine a bit about themselves, their wages,

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.349
and their work conditions.

00:02:48.349 --> 00:02:52.209
Sometimes they showed their horrific injuries
and described what happened, like this boy

00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:56.709
from Bessemer City, North Carolina, whose
hand got crushed in the gears of a cotton

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:58.069
spinner.

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:03.520
We know that because each photo, numbering
over 5,000, includes a detailed caption written

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:04.480
by Hine.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.770
Hine coined the term “photo stories” to
describe this marriage of images and text,

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.380
and it’s a big part of how the photos humanized
the lives of child laborers to an indifferent

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:19.510
public.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:23.050
But it’s also his photographic technique
that makes them feel so personal.

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:26.989
Let’s use the photos of cotton mill workers
like Sadie as an example.

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:31.920
First, many of these photos are framed the
exact same way, just substituting a different

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:32.920
worker.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.860
Hine was trying to show that each child’s
experience was part of a widespread problem,

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:39.410
and the repetition in the images signals that.

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:42.989
You can really see how intentional the framing
is when you look at how the image of Sadie

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:47.110
appeared when it was first published in a
Progressive magazine, in 1909.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:52.099
It’s opposite a nearly identical photo of
a different worker, set so that the symmetry

00:03:52.099 --> 00:03:55.790
of the two images makes the machinery seem
to go on and on.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:57.840
The left-hand caption says, “Spinner.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.170
A type of many in the mill.”

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:04.459
Hine’s photographs are also shot with a
very shallow depth of field, which basically

00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:08.580
means a narrow part of the photo is in focus,
and the rest is blurred out.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:13.500
A photo with a deep depth of field would look
like this one by Jacob Riis, who was photographing

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.109
New York City slums around the same time as
Hine.

00:04:16.109 --> 00:04:20.900
Notice how the playground in the background
is in focus, just like the kids in the foreground.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.820
Now look at Hine’s portraits.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:29.770
In this one, the factory this boy works at
looms behind him, but it’s almost totally

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:30.770
blurred out.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:35.729
This was a recurring visual theme — to include
the machinery or the workplace in the frame,

00:04:35.729 --> 00:04:38.610
but obscure it, favoring the worker instead.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:42.689
This narrow point of focus, combined with
shooting from a lower angle — the eye level

00:04:42.689 --> 00:04:51.020
of these children — is why these images
are so effective at humanizing their subjects.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:54.279
Photos like the ones from the South Carolina
cotton mills changed the public perception

00:04:54.279 --> 00:04:56.400
of child labor in the United States,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.669
ultimately pressuring state legislatures to
introduce laws regulating work for those under

00:05:00.669 --> 00:05:04.499
the age of 18 — and sending kids back to
school.

00:05:04.499 --> 00:05:09.020
Lewis Hine went on to photograph the construction
of the Empire State Building in New York City,

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:13.129
using the same dignifying techniques he photographed
child laborers with:

00:05:13.129 --> 00:05:15.349
Considering the perspective of his subjects

00:05:15.349 --> 00:05:19.990
with a narrow focus, emphasizing the worker,
not the machinery.

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:24.449
Hine was one of the first to use a camera
as a tool for social documentary, to shine

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:27.150
a light on the mostly unseen.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:30.990
He understood early on the power images have
to tell stories.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:32.949
As he said in that 1909 speech:

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:36.689
“Take the photograph of a tiny spinner in
a Carolina cotton mill.

00:05:36.689 --> 00:05:41.300
With a picture thus sympathetically interpreted,
what a lever we have for the social uplift.”

